# Stage 1's rejection fixture: a real rules file for a German bank export, and
# a clear mismatch for checking.csv.
#
# It is deliberately RIGHT about everything stage 1 can check except one thing.
# Four fields against checking.csv's four columns, so the column test passes; the
# only reason to reject it is that %d.%m.%Y cannot read 01/15/2024. That isolates
# the date-format check — a test that passed because the column counts differed
# would not be testing what it claims to.
#
# It is a rules file hledger accepts, which `just rules-check` proves by driving
# it against a generated statement in the format it was written for. Driving it
# against checking.csv would fail, and that failure is precisely what stage 1
# exists to reach WITHOUT spawning hledger at all.

skip 1
fields date, description, amount-out, amount-in
date-format %d.%m.%Y
currency EUR

account1 assets:bank:girokonto
account2 expenses:unknown

if GEHALT
    account2 income:salary

if SUPERMARKT
    account2 expenses:food:groceries
